Read! You can read a textbook in like 4-7 dedicated days. You can go from knowing nothing about a topic to having a really strong understanding.

Take notes while you read, it's okay if you never revisit your notes, you will honestly remember what you wrote, but at least you now that these ideas have hit your brain at least twice (when you first read them and when your wrote them down).

In like middle school, maybe I'd read a book's words aloud internally and consider a page read, but you must get over that.

What's the point in that? It is the internal processing that counts as "reading". You seriously want to tie connections to what you are reading, take a break between paragraphs or chapters, what did you just learn? This analysis is the most important part.

I do wish I could just download information, like a LLM, but I can't, and reading is the closest to quickly digesting concepts and ideas.

I rarely read nonfiction, becuase I'd rather watch a movie, but there are probably tons of ficitonal characters who've faced and overcome dilemmas that I will face in my own future. I should have learned from them rather than forcing myself to repeat their lessonry.

Sometimes even just reading the chapter titles in the table of contents will inform you on a topic. What am I signing up to learn?

There are books on EVERYTHING! Reading is a cheat code. I fear the person who has outread me, the same way I would never want to curve grading in school against someone who's spent all weekend studying.

In biz, you want to KNOW more than your competitors.

Reading is just a means to increasing your personal leverage in the marketplace.
